mt7915: CR6608 MURU recovery-lifecycle attribution

Verified defects in the disarm / strike / re-arm lifecycle (patches 06/08),
found by enumerating every caller of the mask primitives against the
recovery state machine and the mt7996 / upstream reset gates:

1. mt7915_reset() disarmed (with a strike) for every MT_MCU_CMD_ERROR_MASK
   event, including the RESET_DONE / RECOVERY_DONE / NORMAL_STATE handshake
   acknowledgements that upstream and mt7996 ignore.  One such event outside
   a STOP_DMA cycle cleared an armed mask with no re-arm until the next real
   recovery.  The mask is now touched only by the states that start a
   recovery (watchdog -> latch, STOP_DMA / STOP_DMA_FW_RELOAD -> disarm).

2. Strikes were charged for recoveries during which the uplink scheduler
   was never exercised: after a reload the mask is re-armed before
   mac80211 replays the interfaces, so a post-load loop of failing
   mt7915_run commands latched permanently after three rounds with zero
   UL-bearing records ever accepted by the firmware.  A disarm now records
   whether an uplink-bearing STA_REC was acknowledged since the last re-arm
   (cr6608_muru_exercised) and charges a strike only in that case.

3. Three host-initiated recoveries (the regulatory reconfig replay, a
   config call while a band waits for its post-reset witness, a 38 dBm
   request rejected while TX was paused) charged strikes although they are
   re-runs of the handshake, not observed firmware faults.  They now use
   mt7915_mcu_schedule_full_recovery_host(): same recovery, no strike.

4. The strike decay ran only from band 0's mac_work and was blocked by
   band 0's MT76_RESET bit, which after a full reset stays set on a band
   with no open interface until that band's own set_channel runs (never,
   for a 5 GHz-only configuration).  The same sticky bit parked every live
   record replay.  Decay now runs from every phy (idempotent) and both the
   decay and the replay use the recovery definition (MCU_RESET, restart,
   generation) plus the PEER's own band state; mac_work re-kicks parked
   replays within one tick of the window closing.

5. An operator-initiated full chip reset through the fw_ser debugfs knob
   reused the watchdog state bits and therefore spent the permanent latch.
   It is now flagged (cr6608_operator_reset) and attributed to the
   operator: disarm, re-arm after the verified reset.

6. The debugfs mask writer lowered the live mask before the ceiling (a
   disarm + re-arm cycle between the two resurrected the retired bit) and
   validated against the live mask (so during a disarm window only 0 was
   accepted).  It now lowers the ceiling first, validates against the
   ceiling, and reduces the live mask with old & val.

7. dev->muru_debug survived a firmware reload while the firmware's TXC
   trigger-statistics enable did not, so muru_stats accumulated zero
   deltas for the rest of the uptime.  The enable is re-sent after
   mt7915_mac_restart when it was on.

Log wording no longer claims a re-arm that a latch, an exhausted strike
budget or a zero ceiling will refuse, nor that a latch cleared anything in
the firmware (a partial reset keeps firmware records until the replay).
New host telemetry: exercised_since_rearm, operator_reset_pending,
candidate_ul_armed_host_only.  No new MCU command, no ABI change.

